# /usr/lib/crontab -- what /etc/cron runs, and when.  Task C22; cmd/cron/README.md.
#
# Fields are minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week (0-6, SUNDAY IS 0 -- cron.8
# said 1-7 with 1=monday and was wrong about v7's own code), then the command.  A line
# cron cannot parse is dropped in silence, and that is what makes a `#' comment work.
#
# 0644 AND ROOT'S IS PART OF THE DESIGN: cron does not setuid(1) here, so every command
# below runs as root -- which gives nothing away precisely because nobody but root can
# write this file.  It is also what lets the live line work at all: /usr/lib/atrun needs
# suser() for the setuid(2) that gives a job its submitter's identity.
#
# v7 HAD THREE LINES AND ONE IS COMMENTED OUT:
#
#   dmesg wants a /usr/adm that root.manifest deliberately has not got -- the same
#   directory process accounting was refused for in cmd/README.md.  Note also that v7's
#   form is wrong here twice more: this image keeps the program in /bin (task C8), and
#   its `-' flag is gone with the /usr/adm/msgbuf machinery, cmd/dmesg being main(void).
#   So the line to restore is `/bin/dmesg >>/usr/adm/messages', not v7's.
#
# THE CALENDAR LINE IS LIVE AS OF TASK C23, and what unblocked it was task C28 rather
# than C23 itself: `calendar -' mails each user his own reminders, and until /bin/mail
# existed there was nowhere for the letter to go.  It reads $HOME/calendar for every
# line of /etc/passwd and mails nobody who has not written one, so on this image it is
# silent until somebody does.  cmd/calendar/README.md.

#0,10,20,30,40,50	*	*	*	*	/bin/dmesg >>/usr/adm/messages
0,5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55	*	*	*	*	/usr/lib/atrun
20	1	*	*	*	/usr/bin/calendar -
